    - Ferna G. Halliday: Queen's University graduate, YWCA Montreal secretary (1918-1921), responsible for girls at Camp Oolahwan, co-directed Ouareau 1922-1923, founded Camp Oconto 1924, ran Oconto until 1949, OCA founding member (1933).
    - Camp Ouareau property acquired 1921 from Mr. Leguerrier — women needed fathers' permission under Quebec law.
    - Camp Ouareau director succession: Jamieson (1924-?), Percival (1934-1954, bought site 1937), Bertram (1954-?), Raill (1994-present).
    - Camp Oolahwan: Founded 1917 by Mary Susanna Edgar on Lake Walfred, Sainte-Marguerite-du-Lac-Masson. Operated 1917-2005. Longest YWCA initiative.
    - Oolahwan centennial at Camp Ouareau: 2017 (dates conflict: Sept 8-10 per YWCA vs Aug 25-27 per West Island Blog).
    - Camp Ouareau & Glen Bernard Camp (both 1922) = two oldest girls-exclusive camps still operating in Canada.
    - Camp Ouareau changed Indigenous team names (Iroquois, Algonquin, Micmac) in 2014 — parallel to Kanawana's own naming evolution.
    - 1941 forest fire near Lac Ouareau threatened but did not damage Camp Ouareau.
    - YWCA claim that Oolahwan was "first girls camp in Canada" disputed — Camp Northway (1906) predates it.
  LOOP DECISION: CONCLUDE — good coverage on Camp Ouareau, Oolahwan, Halliday, and Oconto. Jamieson biography remains thin (no independent corroboration of YMCA role beyond Wikipedia/Ouareau site). Archival research needed.
  New sources: src_ywca_oolahwan_page, src_ywca_oolahwan_centennial, src_camp_oconto_about, src_ourkids_ouareau, src_qctonline_oolahwan, src_westislandblog_oolahwan
