2026-08-14 | RESEARCH + WRITE | Traditions and programs from the season reports
  Second agent's haul applied. Highlights:
  - CHOPSY: whole-corpus null across 634 text items (~870KB of Kanawana material,
    1921-1993). Far stronger than the earlier 38-issue Green Triangle null.
    BUT: a detail inside the oral legend checks out -- "the Cave", the Hike and
    Trip equipment room the story's axes are stolen from, is documented as
    exactly that in the 1987 director's report. The story is embedded in real
    camp geography. New question for the oral-history instrument: is Chopsy
    connected to the Haunted House, which is documented 1928-1940 and stops
    just before Chopsy's stated 1945 origin?
  - COEDUCATION: 1968, settled three ways (the 1988 report's "66 girls
    registered", the camper-weeks tables, and the 1970 report's "third year as
    a coeducational camp"). The Facts sheet's 1969 is an error, and the phased
    1968-pilot/1969-formalization reading is superseded. The real finding is
    that coeducation was RECOMMENDED IN 1965 WITH COSTINGS AND DECLINED by the
    Metropolitan Board -- a three-year delay, not a gradual drift.
  - A CAMPER DROWNED IN 1969. Recorded in the annual report, unnamed, no date
    or circumstance. The only known camper fatality in the camp's documented
    history, and it appeared nowhere in this wiki. Now in postwar-gap.md.
  - SECTIONS: gender reassignment happened immediately in 1969, not in the
    1970s -- which answers a question the wiki had pinned on a paywalled 1974
    Gazette clipping. Rangers die 1967, Pioneers and Coureurs de Bois 1972, so
    the latter two names in use today are revivals. No document states the 1959
    renaming happened at all; it is bracketed 1958-59. "Rover" is a job title.
  Facts 2057 -> 2069, KB v5.25.

