2026-08-28T02:30:00Z | p_293 COMPLETE (premise corrected) | Created wiki/traditions/knights-of-kanawana.md carrying the full 29-name roll 1980-2009 recovered by p_291, with cross-references to every other plaque each knight appears on. IMPORTANT SELF-CORRECTION: this task existed because my p_291 audit note claimed the Knights appeared in the wiki "nowhere except passing mentions in traditions-and-culture.md and order-of-owens.md". FALSE. traditions-and-culture.md already carried a thorough treatment -- charter text, oath, the six challenges, the birchbark medium, and the 1979-vs-1980 founding question. I made that claim from a GREP HIT rather than from reading the section the grep found, which is precisely the failure mode the plaque audit exists to correct, committed while writing up the plaque audit. Correction recorded in f_1565, in the article's research note, and in p_293's own queue notes. SCOPE ADJUSTED ACCORDINGLY: rather than duplicate the existing section, split it on the hub-and-article pattern already used for the Order of Owens -- traditions-and-culture.md keeps the charter treatment and now links out; the new article carries the roll and the membership analysis. NEW ANALYSIS (f_2342): 29 investitures across 13 distinct years, ~1/year against several hundred campers a season, which bears out the charter's "select group" claim; SIX LADIES of 29 with two among the four inaugural 1980 knights, so the order was gender-inclusive in language and practice from year one, about a decade into coeducation; large gaps (1983-86, 1989-91, 1995-97, 2000-01, 2003-07) that the board cannot disambiguate between "nobody qualified", "the order lapsed" and "the roll is incomplete" -- the 1988 report's "still in effect" against the 1989-91 silence is the sharpest test; and the finding that the order honoured CAMPERS many of whom returned as staff, making the roll an early rung on the career ladder measured in f_2335 rather than a terminal honour. KB v5.67 -> v5.68, 2303 -> 2304 facts.
2026-08-28T03:00:00Z | p_295 + p_296 COMPLETE | Operator supplied the Rangers/Wolf/Trailblazers/Foresters lineage unprompted, which CORRECTED one of my inferences and resolved a gap the wiki had recorded as unresolvable. (1) WOLF IS A PROGRAMME, NOT A STAFF ROLE. The p_291 audit read the 2009 boards' "WOLF" credit as a third staff title. It is the Rangers programme under its later name -- Rangers revived 2001, renamed Wolf later that decade, lapsed again -- so a Wolf on a trip board is a programme participant, exactly as "L.I.T. Daphne Pungartnik" (f_1709) and "Lit Amy D." (f_1597) are on two boards the SAME audit had already transcribed. The evidence for the correct reading was in my own records; an unfamiliar word inside a list of familiar job titles read as another job title. f_2345 records the correction. CONSEQUENCE CARRIED THROUGH: Jeff Ware counted as a junior-to-staff progression only via that misclassification, so f_2335 and wiki/people/multi-year-index.md now read 44, not 45, and scripts/plaque/build_index.py is fixed at source with the reasoning in its docstring. (2) TRAILBLAZERS IS NOT UNDATED. section-names.md stated Trailblazers and Foresters "remain entirely undated ... only in current (2026) YMCA program pages". Trailblazers IS the CIT programme renamed, continuous from inception -- the search that found nothing pre-1980s was looking for the wrong word. Foresters is genuinely recent. Corrected in place; f_2344. (3) NEW ARTICLES: wiki/site/named-places-and-camp-vocabulary.md (the Sanctum and its 2007 epitaph, the Suez Bridge, Frogs Bog, trip destinations, the section codes SB/JG/B/SGC decoded, and the documentary evidence for international campers/staff) and wiki/traditions/plaque-culture.md (the Frost epigraph, the thirteen 1985 catchphrases, the 1975 War-single motto and mascots, the dated 2006 setlist, Kanawoodstock across three objects, the themed-day format, Raku the camp dog inferred from the board's bone shape and paw print, and the two unmixed