2026-09-05T00:00:00Z  RESEARCH  p_368 closed -- Seton's ceremony read and set beside Kanawana's, and the descent turns out to be a text rather than a resemblance. Eastaugh named Seton in 1973; nobody here had opened Seton. The Book of Woodcraft and Indian Lore (1912) was downloaded from the Internet Archive and read for the ceremony sections. FOUR FINDINGS. (1) The PEACE PIPE CEREMONY, pp. 148-149: the Medicine Man opens council, lights the fire with a drill "even as Wakonda himself doth light his fire", and says "Now know we that Wakonda, whose dwelling is above the Thunder-bird, whose messenger is the Thunder-bird, hath been pleased to smile on his children, hath sent down the sacred fire"; then the pipe to Wakonda, to Maka Ina and to the four winds, with "Noon-way" answered to each. Kanawana's own Green Triangle of 21 July 1940 describes the same act in one line: "an Indian brave came into view; he prayed to Wakonda to light the fire" (f_4854). (2) THE CLOSING CHANT HAS A PRINTED CREDIT LINE. At p. 145 Seton prints "THE OMAHA TRIBAL PRAYER, harmonized by Prof. J. C. Fillmore" -- "Wa-kon-da dhe-dhu Wa-pa-dhin a-ton-he" -- credited "(By permission from Alice C. Fletcher's Indian Story and Song)" and translated "Father a needy one stands before thee; I THAT sing am he". Kanawana's script closes "Wakonda, dhe-dhu, wapdhin atonhe"; Eastaugh remembered "I WHO sing am he", one word adrift after fifty years of singing. The line of descent is now end to end: an Omaha prayer, taken down by Alice Fletcher and published in 1900, harmonised by Fillmore, reprinted by Seton in 1912, taught to Canadian camp leaders by Taylor Statten in the 1920s, sung at a Laurentian boys' camp by 1927 -- five hands between the people whose prayer it was and the boys singing it, none of them Omaha (f_4855). (3) C. R. BLACKSTOCK'S MAZINAW FIREPLACE CARVING IS SETON'S SENTENCE WITH THE TENSES CHANGED: it keeps the messenger-Thunderbird clause, the smiling on his children and the sending down of the sacred fire, turns a statement into a petition, drops the dwelling. Not an allusion -- which settles that the three camps' shared Wakonda material is ONE text, not three independent borrowings (f_4856). (4) WHAT THE RITE IS MADE OF: Seton's ceremony mixes Lakota address and pipe invocations (Neetah Kola nayhoon-po, Maka Ina, Wazi-yata, Noon-way) with an Omaha song from a different nation and a different ethnographer's fieldwork, and the "Departure of Hiawatha" in Eastaugh's list of standard parts is neither -- it is Longfellow, 1855, absent from the Book of Woodcraft entirely. Three sources and one poet, performed as a single Indigenous rite (f_4857). CHANGED: council-ring.md gains "The source text, read at last" and the Mazinaw finding (18 -> 19 sources, one RALPH loop, 4,869 -> 5,527 words); indigenous-names-and-land.md gains the five-hands provenance and the composite point (30 -> 31 sources). One source record added for Seton. p_421 opened: ask the Taylor Statten Camps whether Eastaugh's offered "complete and detailed description of the rituals" survives, since that is the version Kanawana's leaders were actually taught. KB v6.32 -> v6.33, 4,819 facts, 1,579 sources. All six verify scripts pass.
