2026-09-05T00:00:00Z  RESEARCH  The Year Books already downloaded for p_405 were read for Manitoba, and three things came out. (1) THE MAN WHO FOUND CAMP STEPHENS'S ISLANDS WAS THE YMCA'S OWN PROVINCIAL CHAIRMAN. The 1893 volume lists "CANADIAN NORTHWEST. Headquarters, Winnipeg, Man. R. D. Richardson, Win'p'g, Man., Chairman" and the 1895 the same for "CANADIAN WEST", and he signs the province's narrative report in both -- "ROBERT D. RICHARDSON, Cor. Mem." (1893), "ROBT. D. RICHARDSON" (1895). The party rowing the Lake of the Woods for a site in 1893 was led by the chairman of the YMCA's own committee for the Canadian Northwest, in the year T. Duncan Patton was about to arrive in Winnipeg. It also settles the initial: Camp Stephens's own page gives R.D., the Lake of the Woods Museum gives R.C.; the contemporaneous printed record gives D, twice (f_4860). (2) A LEAD AND NOT MORE: the 1895 committee lists GEORGE FLEMING OF BRANDON beside Richardson and John Norris, and one of the five islands is named for a "Mr. Fleming" nobody has identified. Fleming is a common name and no source connects the two; written down so whoever reaches the Winnipeg records knows which Fleming to check first (f_4861). (3) THE YEAR BOOKS CANNOT ADJUDICATE c_063. Their Manitoba and Canadian West reports for 1893, 1895 and 1897 describe conventions, secretaries, buildings and college associations and mention no camp at all; "Lake of the Woods" does not occur in any of the five volumes. The same books that fail to record Kanawana's 1894 season fail to record whatever was on those islands, so they cannot prefer 1891 over the chapel service's 1911 or the reverse. The Winnipeg association's own annual reports remain the route, and c_063's notes now say so (f_4862). CHANGED: t-duncan-patton.md 12 -> 13 sources; oldest-camp-question.md records the eliminated route. KB v6.36 -> v6.37, 4,824 facts. All six verify scripts pass.
2026-09-05T00:00:00Z  WRITE  p_336 largely done, in two parts. FIRST, THE PREMISE HAD ALREADY MOVED: a later pass had removed the "modern successor to the QCA" framing the priority was written against, and canadian-camping-movement.md already said "the ACQ was never simply the QCA renamed". SECOND, WHAT WAS STILL MISSING IS NOW IN: Tony Shorgan's own account, "Birth of the French Camping Association (l'Association des Camps du Quebec)", Canadian Camping Vol. 17 No. 3, April 1965, read in full and written in with his dates -- 1957, the meeting called at his own office on twenty-four hours' notice, "despite one of the worst blizzards we have ever had", eighteen French-speaking camp directors attending with Nan Vail; 12 April 1958, the Windsor Hotel session and a committee for a year's trial under his presidency, taking the name Association des Camps Canadiens Francais; 17 July 1958, at his own Crippled Children's Camp at St. Alphonse under the honorary presidency of Monsignor J. A. Papineau, Bishop of Joliette, resolving to apply to the QCA; 15 November 1958, permanent officers after a full day in the Mont St-Louis gymnasium; 1959, a newsletter every two months and inter-camp visits, French directors visiting English and Jewish camps; 1960, THE NAME CHANGE TO "ASSOCIATION DES CAMPS DU QUEBEC (SECTION FRANCAISE)" (f_4863). THE NAMING POINT THE PRIORITY ASKED FOR IS NOW STATED: the association that accredits Kanawana carries the FRENCH SECTION'S name, adopted by that section for itself in 1960 before the merger with the QCA Inc. (English Section) -- a different descent from "the QCA renamed". Three founding dates are set side by side as answers to different questions: QCA 1937 as a chartered CCA unit; French section 1958, named Association des Camps du Quebec 1960; and the ACQ's own current website, "Fondee en 1961 a l'initiative de directeurs de camps de vacances", which is the year of the provincial charter and of the section joining the QCA (f_4864, f_4859). TWO DISCREPANCIES RECORDED AND LEFT STANDING: Shorgan remembers eighteen directors at the blizzard meeting, the June 1958 Quebec report says "some fifteen persons attended"; and he places that meeting in his office in 1957 while the 1958 report gives it no date or place and puts Rene St-Onge in the chair. Compatible if they are different events, which is the natural reading of his own sequence, but the 1958 report does not say so and this is a seven-year-later recollection against a contemporaneous minute (f_4865). The surname appears as SHORGAN in his own byline and the 1959 report, SHORGUN in Winter 1987; the byline is the better authority. A FOOTNOTE-LABEL SLIP CAUGHT IN THE SAME EDIT: the new source was first labelled 8t, which already existed (Plewman), and a careless global replace re-pointed two existing ^8t markers at it. Both restored; the committed diff is purely additive, 38 insertions and no deletions. STILL TO DO: the Tony Shorgan stub -- 23 facts in this KB already mention him and the material clears the threshold several times over, but it needs its own pass rather than a paragraph. canadian-camping-movement.md gains 557 words. KB v6.37 -> v6.38, 4,827 facts. All six verify scripts pass.
2026-09-05T00:00:00Z  WRITE  p_336 closed -- wiki/people/tony-shorgan.md created, as a DRAFT rather than a stub: six sources, his own dated chronology, and a career across three decades of the provincial association. He was Executive Secretary of the Province of Quebec Society for Crippled Children and Camp Director of its camp at St-Alphonse-de-Joliette, a QCA member from about 1943, first president of the French section from 1958, QCA vice-president by 1959, and by 1975 a Past President and Honorary Member. The article carries his 1957-1960 chronology as a table, the two frank passages he chose to print (why the merger waited -- "some of the Camp Directors might be handicapped because they did not understand the English language" -- and the director who told him he needed nobody to dictate to him and later became president of the section), his own camp of 280 boys aged two to twenty-one, and Ken Willis's 1987 note that he was still the man laying plans for Quebec camping's fiftieth. NO KANAWANA CONNECTION IS ASSERTED: Open Question 3 states plainly that he and Kanawana's directors sat in the same association for twenty years and appear in the same officer lists, and that no source puts them in a room together. He is in people/ because the association he built accredits Kanawana and this wiki's account of that association's descent now rests on his testimony. Linked from canadian-camping-movement.md and directors-index.md; 105 -> 106 articles. ONE PROCESS FAILURE RECORDED, in f_4863 and in the re-read notes on f_3146 and f_3147: I read the April 1965 article and extracted it without first searching the KB, which had already extracted the same article as f_3146, f_3147, f_3148 and f_3149. Nothing is deleted -- the duplicate is kept, cross-linked in both directions, and says what it adds (the byline, the fuller quotations, the 1960 renaming, the fifteen years of membership). The lesson is written into it: the KB is the index to the corpus, and a source should be searched for in it BEFORE it is read, not after. citation_aim caught one thin citation in the new article on the first run and it was reworded. All six verify scripts pass.
