2026-09-06T12:00:00Z  VERIFY p_328 -- A. Ross Seaman's 1962-63 secretaryship resolved: NATIONAL (Canadian Camping Association), not provincial. The list is the CCA masthead headed "2233 Belgrave Avenue, Montreal 28, Quebec", and 4140 Jean Renou is the address printed in it. Mastheads read Dec 1961 (Agnes Mutchler, Corresponding Secretary), Feb 1962 and Jun 1962 (Seaman), Feb 1963 (Seaman), Apr 1963 (office moved to Calgary, Eleanor Logan of the Edmonton YMCA, under president Dr Don Smith). No QCA officer list appears in any cached issue between Dec 1961 and Jun 1963; "Quebec Camping Association" occurs once in those eight issues, in a convention notice; and Apr 1962 and Dec 1962 carry no masthead page in the digitized text at all, so those two months can be read neither way. The provincial post is unproven, not disproven, and the article says so. wiki/people/a-ross-seaman.md corrected in place with the wrong sentence quoted (15 -> 16 sources); f_4928 added; f_2471 carries a [CORRECTED 2026-09-06] note. KB 6.65 -> 6.66 (4890 facts). All seven verify scripts pass.
2026-09-06T12:30:00Z  WRITE p_342 + p_381 -- both were stale in the queue, done on 2026-09-04 and never marked, and both left something behind. p_342: camp-oolahwan.md already named May Brown, and since then had acquired two FIRST-HAND accounts dating her to 1945-1947, but the paragraph built on Ecclestone's undated 1966 profile still called the dating unconfirmed inference and Open Question 8 still called her years unfixed. Both corrected; Open Question 8 now also names Marjorie A. Booth and states the two spans still blank, 1919-1945 and 1948-1979. p_381: the separate accreditation of Les Voyageurs de la Verendrye was already in canoe-trips.md and between-centennials.md, but the gender comparison was nowhere -- Kanawana 90 boys / 90 girls at exact parity against Les Voyageurs 15 boys / 5 girls, same director, same table. Written into coeducation-gender.md with a new [dir75] source entry, and that article's sources_cited refreshed (eight ids it cites in brackets were missing). No new facts; f_3816, f_2287, f_4257, f_4259 and f_4727 already carried all of it. All seven verify scripts pass.
2026-09-06T13:00:00Z  WRITE p_386 (+ p_385 verified stale) -- the 1976 YMCA-to-RLSS lifesaving changeover written into programs-activities.md from Doug Dent's January 1976 column: the changeover sentence, the division of labour between RLSS, Red Cross and YMCA, the full three-column equivalence chart, and the age floors. Fact f_4929. The usable rule is that a Star level dates a swimmer to the YMCA scheme and a Life Saving I/II/III dates them after the RLSS renumbering. Byproduct: the article already held Kanawana's 1923 KLS as keyed to the RLSS Award of Merit, half a century before the YMCA nationally did the same thing -- written up with the caveat that nothing here says what the camp taught in between. p_385 checked line by line against derek-walsh.md and found already complete; marked without changes. A string-match staleness sweep over all 94 pending priorities carrying quotations was built and run and is NOT trustworthy: its top-scoring hits are archive-access items whose quoted phrases are file titles that naturally appear in the wiki, and it missed p_385, which is genuinely stale. Recorded as a null; hand-checking the WRITE-type priorities is what actually worked. KB 6.66 -> 6.67 (4891 facts). All seven verify scripts pass.
2026-09-06T13:30:00Z  WRITE p_387 -- the three structural breaks of 1974-1976 written into meta/sources-index.md ahead of the production table: the February 1974 suspension, the November 1975 four-page newsletter forced by the mail strike, and the June 1976 "this issue is its last" editorial, each verified against the cached issue rather than taken from the priority. Two things the priority did not have. The November 1974 explanation continues into the Task Force's recommendation that the January issue become a DIRECTORY ISSUE -- so the 1975 and 1976 directories, this project's source for Kanawana's capacity, its director, its activities and the separate accreditation of Les Voyageurs de la Verendrye, exist because of the association's worst financial year. And the June 1976 editorial's mention of an April issue invites the inference that a number is missing: Vol. 28 is numbered 1, 2, 3, 4 consecutively, so nothing is absent from the cache, and the April 1976 number was planned rather than published. Fact f_4930. KB 6.67 -> 6.68 (4892 facts). All seven verify scripts pass.
2026-09-06T14:00:00Z  WRITE p_357 -- the CCA's 1971 regional restructuring written into canadian-camping-movement.md, placed ahead of the 1985 "money stops" section because it corrects it. Latimer's summer 1971 letter with the five regional vice-presidents, and Hartry's "C.C.A. -- New National Office" of 14 December 1970 with the $45.00-a-month OCA arrangement and the sentence that project grants remained available and administrative grants did not. New source note 8bs. TWO FINDINGS BEYOND THE PRIORITY. The Summer 1969 issue reports the Ottawa national office opening on a grant from the Fitness and Amateur Sports Branch, announced at the annual meeting of 28 February 1969, with a paid Executive Secretary, Barry Bryant -- so February 1969 to January 1971, under two years, exactly as the priority said and against my own first reading of Hartry's memo, which only rejects a proposed office. And that contradicts this article's own paragraph from Jocelyn Palm's winter 1985 editorial, which dates the office and its federal funding to 1971: on the contemporaneous record the office is two years older and federal administrative money stopped rather than started around then. CONFLICT c_064 raised (two documented sources, human decision point); both are now printed beside each other. Also written: Quebec's standing regional vice-presidency, the seat this article follows through Lazanik, Walsh, Belanger and Willis, exists because the travelling provincial executive ended in 1971 -- and the same travelling executive is why Seaman could be national secretary from a house in Lachine in 1962. All seven verify scripts pass (one caught a header count: a lettered sub-entry is not a numbered source).
2026-09-06T14:30:00Z  WRITE p_367 -- the movement's own 1972-73 "Threat to Camping" frame written into between-centennials.md, placed before the 1978 Granek findings because it is the earlier frame: Latimer's autumn 1972 letter, the Geneva Park National Camp Directors Workshop of 1-4 February 1973 and its title, and the 1972 unemployment-insurance change with its particulars and the $30.00-a-week threshold. Two of the priority's own items were NOT written: the Ontario sales tax, which does not reach a Quebec camp, and a "1.4 times the employee's rate" figure that is not in the passages read. Two consequences stated: Kanawana's payroll costs rose in 1972 by an amount set in Ottawa, at the front end of the decade in which its record thins, so the thinning, the 1978 Quebec enrolment findings and this are three views of one squeeze; and from 1972 every Kanawana counsellor and instructor generated federal employment paper, a body of record outside the YMCA's own files that this project has never looked for. p_366 annotated with that. Fact f_4931. KB 6.68 -> 6.69 (4893 facts). All seven verify scripts pass.
2026-09-06T15:00:00Z  WRITE p_297 -- cohort persistence written into cit-lit-program.md as "Cohorts moved together, not one by one", with every count RE-DERIVED from kb/plaque-audit/person-index.json instead of repeated. Twelve of the 29 CITs of 1995 on the 76-name Staff 97 board; all eight campers of the ten-entry 2000 shower tent board on later boards; seven of the 24 LITs of 2008 on the eighteen-name 2009 JC board. The third count came back SIX and the priority was right: the index had SPLIT Justin Pulfer, painted "Justin 'JT' Pulfer" in 2008 and "Justin J. Pulfer" in 2009, because shape() strips a quoted nickname and keeps a middle initial. Merged in scripts/plaque/build_index.py with the reasoning in a comment; person-index.json and multi-year-index.md regenerated by their builders; 1,475 distinct names to 1,474 and 160 traced people to 161, progressions unchanged at 69. multi-year-index.md's correction history extended with this seventh split and the method that found it: the KB was used as a TEST of the index rather than a consumer of it. No new names printed in the new section, which keeps it inside the C5 constraint in the article's own research note. Fact f_4932. KB 6.69 -> 6.70 (4894 facts). All seven verify scripts pass.
2026-09-06T15:30:00Z  WRITE p_349 (hymn half) -- Mary S. Edgar's "Hymn for the Space-Age", Canadian Camping November 1970, tune Forest Green, written into environmental-history.md as the earliest environmental statement in the national camping press this project holds: eight months before Kanawana's own erosion confession, nine years before "No Trace Camping", by the movement's Honorary President at eighty-one. Two verses quoted; the space frame noted, since the hymn opens on "Infinity of space" and turns from there to the planet; and the limit stated, that no source says any Kanawana camper sang it. Cross-referenced from centennial-1967.md, where "Canada's Our Country" already sat, with the point that the Centennial hymn form outlived the Centennial by three years and became an environmental one. Facts f_3453, f_3454, f_3343 already existed; no new fact needed. STILL OPEN: the 1970 book "Once There Was a Camper", which needs catalogue surfaces not yet tried from here. p_349 marked partially_complete. p_221 annotated as operator-gated -- fourteen names on a privacy list a person drew up case by case is not an agent's call. All seven verify scripts pass.
2026-09-06T16:00:00Z  RESEARCH -- A DEAD END REOPENED, AND THE BEST FIND OF THE SESSION. openlibrary.org/search/inside.json full-text searches the Internet Archive book corpus INCLUDING lending-restricted and print-disabled items, unauthenticated, returning the item identifier, the scan leaf and a ~12-word highlighted window; overlapping phrase queries walk the text outwards, six to ten queries a paragraph. This project recorded on 2026-07-09 that searching inside Fong's McConnell biography was a "genuine dead end" because the Archive's own fulltext/inside.php returns 403 for a print-disabled item -- true of that route, false of the world, and it stood fourteen months. Capability and method at f_4933, with the routes that still fail. THREE FINDINGS IN THE FIRST HOUR. (1) f_4934: Fong, McGill-Queen's 2008, scan leaf 766 -- "1912, in that year he also bought a site at Lake Desjardins, near Saint-Sauveur, for a new Camp Kanawana, which was renamed Lake Wilson after his first son." First attestation of the renaming from outside the institution, a date for it, and the end of conflict c_006, which existed because no lake called Desjardins was documented anywhere. c_006 RESOLVED. (2) f_4935: the entry for LOCKE, ROY in The Canadian Obituary Record -- Roy DOUGLAS Locke, b. Toronto c.1920, d. Montreal West 19 September 1991 aged 71, mayor of Montreal West, YMCA director of financial development to 1986, "and was a director of Camp Kanawana". That joins the mayor and the camp chief roy-locke.md had explicitly refused to identify with each other. One tension kept open: the obituary puts him in business from 1954 while the YMCA's own 1954 report has him at Westmount Branch from 1 September. (3) j-w-mcconnell.md was found to be fifteen months behind its own project on the naming question, still calling it unverified four days after lake-wilson.md recorded the 1951 history's answer; corrected in place and marked as the failure it is. Two reconstructions cached under sources/cache/openlibrary-search-inside/ with the exact queries that produced them, each headed by a statement that it is a reading and not a facsimile. Three source records added or upgraded (1607 -> 1609). Four priorities spawned: p_426 read the rest of Fong; p_427 the six other books naming Kanawana, including a survivor's memoir describing letters written from the camp; p_428 KANAWANA WAS A POST OFFICE, in the 1915 Post Office Department report and the Canadian Almanac tables, which nothing in this wiki says; p_429 a Kanawana director in Who's Who in Canada 1965 whose name was not recovered. Null results logged in project-docs/research-log.md: phrase search cannot see through hyphenated line breaks, which is why "director of Camp Kanawana" returns nothing while the sentence exists. KB 6.70 -> 6.71 (4897 facts). All seven verify scripts pass.
2026-09-06T16:30:00Z  RESEARCH p_428 -- KANAWANA WAS A POST OFFICE OF THE DOMINION OF CANADA, AND A SUMMER ONE. Nothing in this wiki said so. The Post Office Department's annual report for 1914-15 lists it among Non-Accounting Post Offices -- the smallest class, no money-order or savings account, postmaster on an allowance -- with no opened/closed letter prefix, so already running and not closed that year; the same report carries the mail route "Kanawana -- St. Sauveur des Mont[s]" under Mail Transportation, Montreal Division. The Sessional Papers for 1919 give "Kanawana (summer office) P.Q.", the parenthesis being the Department's own and used there for two Ontario cottage-country offices. The Canadian Almanac lists it in "Post Offices and Railroad Stations in Canada" in both the 1917 and 1918 editions. The Atlas of Canada still indexed it as an unincorporated place in 1981. All four read out of full djvu text, not left at the snippet; three source records and three cache files, each headed by what could NOT be read -- the revenue figures and the almanac's railway columns are OCR column runs that cannot be aligned to their rows. Written into the-kanawana-site.md with the point that a place gets a post office when enough mail moves through it to be worth a contract, so the boys' letters home were federal mail on a Dominion route. Footnote added to places-and-locations.md: the earlier assumption that "P.O." on the camp map meant Post Office was wrong about the map and right about the institution, and the map correction stands. Dates, postmaster and catchment remain open; Library and Archives Canada's Post Offices and Postmasters database answers all three and returned HTTP 403 at the gateway, which is an access failure. Fact f_4936. KB 6.71 -> 6.72 (4898 facts), sources 1609 -> 1612. All seven verify scripts pass.
2026-09-06T17:00:00Z  RESEARCH p_427 (two of six) -- two Azrieli Foundation survivor memoirs read out of lending-restricted books through Open Library search-inside. MICHAEL KUTZ, "If, By Miracle": his Knights of Pythias lodge held an annual December party at the MONTREAL CHILDREN'S HOSPITAL "for the children, most of whom were bedridden or in wheelchairs, AS WELL AS FOR DIABETIC CHILDREN FROM CAMP KANAWANA." Nothing in this project records diabetic campers at Kanawana. The phrase reads as a standing group, not an incident, and the KB already holds the context that makes it plausible -- Canada had exactly two diabetic camps in 1954 (f_2673) and Quebec's first ran at Camp Jackson Dodds in August 1958 under a Montreal Children's Hospital medical committee (f_2849), the same hospital Kutz names. Written into programs-activities.md with the two institutions to ask. Fact f_4938. JUDY ABRAMS, "Retenue par un fil": "lorsque je suis partie dans un camp de vacances prive, appele Escobar, il a pour sa part rejoint le camp Kanawana du YMCA. Il m'a ecrit de longues lettres decrivant de maniere detaillee ses voyages en canoe dans la nature sauvage des Laurentides." Abrams was born in Budapest in 1937 and reached Montreal in 1949, so this is the early or mid 1950s -- inside the 1947-58 true postwar gap. Written into postwar-gap.md. HE IS NOT NAMED and the relationship is not stated in the recovered text; nothing inferred. What it establishes is that camper letters from the emptiest decade were written at all, which is the first evidence of that. Fact f_4937. Both reconstructions cached in one file with every query that produced them and a statement of what was NOT recovered. Four books of the six still unread, the most valuable being "Le Quebec en plein air" (2016) on the camp's own identity and a predecessor. KB 6.72 -> 6.73 (4900 facts), sources 1612 -> 1614. All seven verify scripts pass.
2026-09-06T17:30:00Z  RESEARCH p_427 (third of six) -- "Le Quebec en plein air" (Quebec Amerique, 2016) read out, and it is a warning rather than a find. Larue and Belec: "Connu longtemps sous le nom d'Otoreke, ce camp existe toujours: c'est maintenant le camp Kanawana." Right about the institution and wrong about the site -- Kanawana is thirty kilometres from Saint-Adolphe-d'Howard -- and it erases seventy-two years, since Otoreke went on operating at Lac Saint-Joseph until 1982. A reader of that book concludes Otoreke closed in 1910 and Kanawana stands on the islands, and both are false. Written into camp-otoreke.md as a named subsection so the correction travels with the sentence, because a mainstream Quebec press title is the kind of book a researcher reaches first. The same page dates Camp Nominingue to 1911 as the first francophone camp against the QCA's own 1974-75 directory giving 1925; recorded as a query in quebec-camp-landscape.md, not as a correction, since the two may be measuring different things. Fact f_4939, source and cache file with the eight queries and a statement of what is wrong with the passage. camp-otoreke.md's sources_cited was also ten ids short and is refreshed. Three of p_427's six books left. KB 6.73 -> 6.74 (4901 facts), sources 1614 -> 1615. All seven verify scripts pass.
